ROAST.PET FRONTIER STACK SCANNER =E2=80=94 2026-09-20 10:14 Asia/Phnom_Penh

1 USEFUL FIND

PIXVERSE V6 ON FAL =E2=80=94 TEST NOW

Source: https://fal.ai/pixverse-v6
Freshness: fal's V6 endpoint/docs are now live and current; the API
pages were crawled within the last several days and the V6 pricing
snapshot was updated September 19. This is a newly actionable
production API surface rather than merely a marketing mention.

WHAT CHANGED
PixVerse V6 is exposed through fal with image-to-video, text-to-video,
first/last-frame transition, and video extension. It supports 1=E2=80=9315
second output, 360p/540p/720p/1080p, optional native audio, multi-clip
generation, deterministic seeds, and style controls. fal highlights
20+ camera/lens controls and multi-shot storytelling.

WHY ROAST.PET CARES
The unusually useful part is not generic cinematic quality: it is a
cheap image-to-video candidate generator with native audio plus
transition/extension endpoints behind the same API. It gives Roast.pet
another inexpensive contender for animating the canonical pet card
and, importantly, can be used as a cheap motion layer before
exact-audio lip-sync.

INPUTS / OUTPUTS
Image-to-video: canonical PetAsset + prompt -> MP4.
Transition: first image + end image + prompt -> MP4.
Extend: existing MP4 + prompt -> extended MP4.
Text-to-video is also available but less relevant to preserving a
customer's pet.

API / INSTALL
npm install @fal-ai/client, set FAL_KEY, call
fal-ai/pixverse/v6/image-to-video, /transition or /extend. Serverless;
no GPU management.

PRICING
Per output second on fal:
360p: $0.025 no audio / $0.035 audio
540p: $0.035 / $0.045
720p: $0.045 / $0.060
1080p: $0.090 / $0.115
Thus an 8-second 720p silent motion candidate is ~$0.36; with native
audio ~$0.48. A 5-second 360p silent screening candidate is ~$0.125.

COMMERCIAL / PRIVACY
fal marks the relevant V6 image-to-video/transition/extend surfaces
for commercial use. It is hosted, not self-hosted, so upload only
normalized/derived PetAssets where possible and apply the normal
retention/privacy policy.

QUALITY / LATENCY CONSTRAINTS
The API claims character/spatial coherence and strong camera control,
but none of that establishes preservation of pet markings, whiskers,
ear geometry or believable animal mouths. Native dialogue should not
be assumed to beat exact-audio lip-sync. Treat it as a renderer
candidate until the hard-pet corpus says otherwise.

EXACT ROAST.PET USE CASE
Canonical podium card -> PixVerse V6 cheap image-to-video motion ->
pet-specific QC -> exact selected roast audio via dedicated lip-sync
if required -> final AV-QC. Also test first/last-frame transition for
controlled setup-to-punchline motion without asking one model to
invent the whole sequence.

SMALLEST TEST
Use 20 fixed hard pets. For each, generate one 5s 360p and one 5s 720p
image-to-video clip with identical motion instructions. Compare
against the current H3/LongCat/Wan candidates on identity retention,
markings, fur/whiskers, mouth/ear/paw integrity, motion naturalness,
usable-render %, latency and billed cost. For the 5 best pets, test V6
transition using two approved canonical stills to see whether explicit
endpoints constrain identity better than unconstrained image-to-video.

DECISION: TEST NOW. Do not adopt until it wins usable-pet-video-per-dollar.

TOP 5 STACK CHANGES TO TEST NOW
1. Add PixVerse V6 to the fixed hard-pet renderer benchmark,
especially 360p/720p image-to-video and transition.
2. Keep Qwen3.8-Omni-Flash as unified ProductionQC and train
thresholds on deliberate pet-identity failures.
3. Continue H3 Max vs LongCat/Wan/HD-V1 comparison for exact talking-pet ou=
tput.
4. Keep local PetPrep -> fur/edge QC -> hosted fallback before any
paid renderer.
5. N-way TTS -> near-free STT integrity -> ComedyRanker -> animate
only winner -> deterministic AV gates -> multimodal QC -> enhance only
accepted output.

CHEAPEST MEANINGFUL CAPABILITY IMPROVEMENT
PixVerse V6 provides a new cheap screening tier: 5 seconds at 360p
without audio costs about $0.125. If pet identity survives at that
tier, best-of-N motion search can happen cheaply before final
lip-sync/upscale. Local PetPrep remains essentially $0 marginal API
cost.

BIGGEST CAPABILITY SHOCK THIS HOUR
No new foundation-model shock cleared verification. The useful change
is economic: a commercially usable, multi-endpoint video model now
gives us image-to-video + controlled first/last-frame transitions +
extension at $0.025/s starting cost behind one serverless API.

CONCRETE STACK SIMPLIFICATION
Make VideoRenderer capability-based rather than model-based:
PetAsset -> render_motion(image-to-video | transition) -> pet QC ->
selected audio/lip-sync -> AV QC -> enhancer/export.
Use transition when two canonical stills exist; use unconstrained
image-to-video only when needed. This lets the factory swap
PixVerse/H3/Wan/etc. without changing upstream RoastSpec or downstream
QC.

No other fresh, verified Roast.pet-specific release cleared the
inclusion threshold in this run; mature unchanged tools were excluded.
